--- /dev/null
+++ b/ssd-target/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# ssd-target: root patching a non-booted install
+
+Local derivative of OpenCore Legacy Patcher 3.0.0-rc.3 (branch `ssd-target`, base 238a876f) for ssd-tools.
+Stock OCLP patches the booted system. This branch patches a macOS install that has never booted, from
+another macOS running on the **same machine** (hardware detection still probes the running machine).
+That other macOS can be a recoveryOS on a USB stick (runs 2026-10-19 on) or a booted, OCLP-patched install with
+the target attached, e.g. over USB (the 2026-10-10 prototype on this model: output = image 02-oclp-root-patched).
+Both need root; as root OCLP's privileged helper is bypassed.
+
+```
+sudo <python> ssd-target/run.py --patch_sys_vol --target <volume or diskXsY> [--hooks DIR] [--kdk_dir DIR]
+```
+
+- `--target` (alias `--target_volume`, the prototype's name): any volume of the target's APFS volume group.
+  Refuses the booted container, locked Data volumes, targets older than Big Sur, and a target whose build differs
+  from the running OS (`--allow_build_mismatch` overrides; untested). The target's live System volume is mounted at
+  `/private/var/tmp/oclp-target/<dev>` (a leftover there is reused only if it is a read/write mount of the target),
+  its Data volume where DiskArbitration mounts it. OS version, build and Darwin version come from the target.
+  Every exit unmounts the System volume again; the Data volume stays mounted.
+- `--target`, `--hooks`, `--kdk_dir`, `--allow_build_mismatch` without `--patch_sys_vol`: exit 2.
+- `--hooks DIR`: run `*.sh` files and `<folder>/hook.sh` (sorted) after OCLP's patch sets and before kmutil + bless.
+  Args: System mount, Data mount; env `OCLP_TARGET_BUILD`, `OCLP_TARGET_VERSION`, `OCLP_PYTHON`. A failing hook
+  stops patching before the snapshot. Also works for the booted system.
+- `--kdk_dir DIR`: Kernel Debug Kits are looked up here instead of `/Library/Developer/KDKs`.
+- `--hardware_profile FILE`: use the hardware recorded in FILE (JSON, format 1) instead of probing the running
+  machine, so the target can be patched from a machine without its GPU or Wi-Fi card (ex. a VM).
+  `Computer.from_profile()` classifies the recorded PCI devices with OCLP's own classes (GPU arch, Wi-Fi chipset);
+  OCLP/OpenCore NVRAM, Secure Boot, SIP and AMFI state are still read from the running machine, so the VM must boot
+  with the target's SIP/AMFI settings. Keys: `format`, `model`, `board_id`, `firmware_vendor`, `cpu {name, flags,
+  leafs}`, `pci [{name, vendor_id, device_id, class_code, model?, acpi_path?, pci_path?}]` (hex strings), optional
+  `ambient_light_sensor`, `pcie_webcam`, `t1_chip`, `third_party_sata_ssd`, `bluetooth_chipset`,
+  `internal_keyboard_type`, `trackpad_type`. Unknown keys are ignored.
+- Exit codes in target mode: 0 patched, 1 patching failed, 2 target not usable / not root.
+- `run.py` stubs wxPython, so a plain Python with `requests packaging py_sip_xnu pyobjc-core
+  pyobjc-framework-Cocoa` is enough (ssd-tools: `handoff/oclp-target/runtime`, x86_64 CPython 3.12.14).
+  Running as root skips OCLP's privileged helper.
+
+Not supported for a target: patch sets with EXECUTE commands (T1, non-Metal enforcement), AuxKC-only rebuilds
+(no KDK), unpatching, and the auto-patch LaunchAgents (skipped).
+
+Test (host, no root, nothing executed):
+`python ssd-target/tests/test_target_mode.py <Universal-Binaries.dmg mount> <work dir>` -
+real patch sets for MacBookPro9,2 on 26.7.1 (25G241), every root command recorded and checked; the
+resulting OCLP record is identical to the master image's (9 sets).
+`--hardware-profile FILE` runs OCLP's real detection on `Computer.from_profile(FILE)` and requires the laptop's 9 sets
+in the laptop run's install order.
+`python ssd-target/tests/test_consolidated.py <work dir>` checks the pieces merged from the prototype (CLI alias and
+guard, WindowServer quoting, mount release, leftover-mount rules, Big Sur minimum, same-build rule).
+
+## Intel KDK overlay
+
+The merge stages a private filtered copy of Extensions: omit debug/development/KASAN files and complete bundles whose executable is arm64e-only; retain the stock destination bundle unchanged. Fat kext executables retain the exact generic x86_64 slice (not x86_64h), including its signature bytes. Source KDK files are never modified. The overlay is merged with rsync --checksum; no delete flag, AMD stock bundles and Kernels stay untouched. Missing rsync or malformed/ambiguous fat input refuses. Temporary staging is removed by its owned context.
+
+Tests: ssd-target/tests/test_kdk_intel_payload.py and test_kdk_trim.py. Owner2026-10-26: no dedicated S3 comparison; next normal full dev build supplies build validation. Previous pipeline pin c67a05315c77badd2246f2f9ecb1be93cc56ad26 is retained for one-step revert.
